course: dictionary
lesson: 11083
title: Which way is west?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: West is a direction, one of the four compass points. An easy way to remember it is the sun. The sun goes down in the west in the evening. «In the morning the Sun is in the east, so shadows point west.» Shadows fall away from the sun, so in the morning they point west.
explain: West can change its job in a sentence. It can be a noun (the west), an adjective (a west wind) or an adverb (head west). Read the words around it to see which one it is. «A west wind blows from the west.» The first west describes the wind, and the second west names the direction.
explain: The West can also name a place, with a capital W, as in the American West. Look for clues like cowboys or the Wild West. Those tell you it means a region, not a direction on a map.
fact: West is one of the four compass points: north, east, south and west.
fact: The sun goes down in the west in the evening.
fact: On a map, north is at the top, so west is on the left.
fact: «Longitude measures how far east or west a place is from the prime meridian, which runs through Greenwich in London.»
fact: The West Coast and the Wild West are phrases that use west as a place name, not just a direction.
exercise: In the evening the sun goes down in the ... | south | east | north | *west
exercise: On a map, which side is west? | bottom | right | top | *left
exercise: In the phrase «the Wild West», what does West mean? | the direction where the sun rises | *a region, often linked to cowboys in stories | a kind of map | a strong wind
test: «In the morning the Sun is in the east, so shadows point ...» | *west | east | south | north
test: Which phrase uses west as a place rather than a direction? | west of the school | a west wind | head west | *the American West
exam: Which sentence uses west as an adjective? | The American West is very big. | Head west until you reach the coast. | The sun goes down in the west. | *A west wind blows across the field.
